        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"austen-persuasion-473794649dc5ac56\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"“I have loved none but you” is principally a truth-conditional avowal of exclusive and enduring love. Within Wentworth’s renewed offer of himself, it also helps enact a relational undertaking, though the explicit performative force lies more directly in the surrounding proposal than in this sentence alone. Its emotional urgency does not establish expressive/reflexive force: the sentence is deliberately written as evidence of constancy, not presented as an uncontrolled verbal discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is how much of the letter’s proposal-like performative force should be attributed to this individual sentence rather than to “I offer myself to you again” and the letter as a whole.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Captain Wentworth writes the avowal in a private letter addressed and delivered to Anne Elliot while urgently responding to the conversation he overhears.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Wentworth’s sentence counters the claim that men’s love dies sooner and assures Anne that, despite his resentment and unjust conduct, he has remained exclusively attached to her. The letter renews his courtship and makes Anne’s response decisive for their future. His agitation, haste, and claim that he can hardly write heighten the avowal’s urgency, but the wording remains a purposeful assertion of constancy rather than a reflex-like discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I have loved none but you.”\",\"supports\":\"Strong truth-conditional force: Wentworth presents his exclusive, continuing love for Anne as true.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I offer myself to you again with a heart even more your own” and “A word, a look, will be enough to decide whether I enter your father’s house this evening or never.”\",\"supports\":\"The avowal occurs within a renewed offer of relationship, giving it moderate contextual performative force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Unjust I may have been, weak and resentful I have been, but never inconstant.”\",\"supports\":\"Frames the core sentence as a considered assertion and defence of enduring constancy.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I can hardly write. I am every instant hearing something which overpowers me.”\",\"supports\":\"Shows intense and immediate emotion but, without evidence of involuntary production, does not independently support expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration identifies the words as a letter directed to “Miss A. E.—” that Wentworth places before Anne.\",\"supports\":\"Establishes the utterance as written and intentionally communicated to Anne.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e6\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is in Jane Austen’s Persuasion, Chapter XXIII, and is tagged as an affirmative exclusive-target perfect construction.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the bibliographic location and the explicit exclusive-love form of the core utterance.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal of exclusive enduring love, while P captures its secondary role in renewing a relational commitment. The urgency and passion are adequately treated as contextual features, so neither E nor O is needed.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
